hypotheses.statement:
For every $K,Y$ as above there is a finite constant $C(K,Y)$ such that $$\|T_\mu-T_
u\|_{L^2(ho)}
 \le C(K,Y)\,W_2(\mu,
u)^{1/3}
 \qquad igl(\mu,
u\in\mathop{\mathrm{Prob}}(Y) igr).$$ The targets may be atomic, singular, or absolutely continuous. The exponent $1/3$ cannot be replaced by any larger exponent in general, even for $K=Y=[-1,1]^d$ and targets with exactly three atoms.

On the open set of configurations with distinct sites and $M_i>0$ for every $i$, the maps $M_i$ and $B_i$ are jointly $C^1$ in the intercepts and sites. For an intercept direction $r=(r_i)$ and a site direction $v=(v_i)$, $$\begin{aligned}
 D M_i[r,v]
 &=\sum_{j\ne i}\bigl[g_{ij}(r_i-r_j)
          +b_{ij}\cdot(v_i-v_j)\bigr],
 \label{cells:mass-differential}\\
 D B_i[r,v]
 &=\sum_{j\ne i}b_{ij}(r_i-r_j)
   +\frac1{|K|}\sum_{j\ne i}\frac1{|y_i-y_j|}
        \int_{F_{ij}}x\bigl(x\cdot(v_i-v_j)\bigr)\,dS(x).
 \label{cells:moment-differential}
\end{aligned}$$ The graph on $\{1,\ldots,N\}$ with edges $g_{ij}>0$ is connected. Consequently, the symmetric operator $$\label{cells:laplacian}
 (Gq)_i=D M_i[q,0]=\sum_{j\ne i}g_{ij}(q_i-q_j)$$ has kernel consisting of the constant vectors and range $\{z\in\mathbb R^N:\sum_i z_i=0\}$.

Fix distinct sites $y_1,\ldots,y_N$ and intercepts $c_1,\ldots,c_N$ whose cells have positive masses $M_i$. If $K\subset\{x:|x|\le R\}$, then every intercept direction $q\in\mathbb R^N$ satisfies $$\sum_{i=1}^N\frac{|D B_i[q,0]|^2}{M_i}
  \le C_0R^2\sum_{i=1}^N\frac{|D M_i[q,0]|^2}{M_i},
  \qquad C_0=162.$$

Set $C_0=162$ and $$\label{gradient:constant}
 C_*=
 \left(12dR^2(1+\sqrt{C_0})^2
             +\frac{28L^2P_K}{|K|}\right)^{1/2}.$$ For any two finitely supported probabilities $\mu,\nu$ on $Y$, $$\|T_\mu-T_\nu\|_{L^2(\rho)}
 \le C_*W_2(\mu,\nu)^{1/3}.$$

